<title>USA: Florida Legislature cuts unemployment tax.</title>
<headline>Florida Legislature cuts unemployment tax.</headline>
<dateline>TALLAHASSEE, Fla. 1997-04-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Florida Legislature has approved a one-year cut in the state's unemployment compensation tax, giving businesses a $162 million break.</p>
<p>The House approved the proposal (CR/HB3) in a 116-1 vote Thursday, a day after it was approved in the Senate. It now goes to Gov. Lawton Chiles, who is expected to sign it.</p>
<p>The proposal cuts the unemployment tax businesses pay by up to $35 per employee for all but those with the worst lay-off recrods.</p>
<p>It also gives recipients a five percent boost in compensation during the first eight weeks of unemployment, and raises the maximim weekly benefit by $25.</p>
<p>Florida's unemployment compensation fund has a $2 billion surplus, enough to pay unemployed workers for three years without any additional contributions from businesses.</p>
<p>((--Jane Sutton, 305-374-5013))</p>
